---
export const prerender = false;

import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../../../styles/etd.css';
import { requireAdmin } from '../../../../lib/admin';

// User manual for the Electronic Trade Documents system.
const gate = await requireAdmin(Astro);
if (gate instanceof Response) return gate;
---
<Layout title="ETD user guide" noindex>
  <article class="wrap etd prose">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/portal/admin/etd">← Electronic Trade Documents</a></p>
    <h1>Electronic Trade Documents: user guide</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      How to issue, pass on, deliver and settle Promissory Notes and Bills of Exchange, and how the system keeps
      them safe from forgery and copying.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <a href="#what">What this is</a>
      <a href="#ideas">Three ideas to understand</a>
      <a href="#start">Quick start</a>
      <a href="#acts">What you can do with an instrument</a>
      <a href="#limits">Limits and the stop</a>
      <a href="#exchange">Exchanging currency</a>
      <a href="#discount">Selling before maturity</a>
      <a href="#keys">Looking after keys</a>
      <a href="#check">Checking an instrument</a>
      <a href="#share">Sharing with a bank or counterparty</a>
      <a href="#ids">Market identifiers</a>
      <a href="#trouble">If something goes wrong</a>
      <a href="#limits">What this does not do</a>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="what">What this is</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>Promissory Note</strong> is a written promise: "I, the maker, will pay this sum to whoever holds this
      note." A <strong>Bill of Exchange</strong> is an order: "I, the drawer, instruct you, the drawee, to pay this
      sum to whoever holds this bill." Both can be passed from one person to another, and whoever holds the
      instrument is owed the money.
    </p>
    <p>
      This system keeps these instruments electronically, in one trusted register, so that each one has exactly one
      real original, a clear current holder, and a tamper-proof history.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ideas">Three ideas to understand</h2>
    <h3>1. The register is the original</h3>
    <p>
      A file can always be copied, so a copy can never prove ownership. Instead, the register records who holds
      each instrument right now. Anything you print or download is only a picture of it. A copy is worth exactly
      what the register says about that instrument today.
    </p>
    <h3>2. A holder key is your signing pen</h3>
    <p>
      Each holder has a <strong>key file</strong>, protected by a passphrase. Passing on, acknowledging payment, or
      changing a key all need the current holder's key file and passphrase. Without it, nobody, including an
      administrator, can move the instrument on that holder's behalf. The key is created in your own browser and
      never sent to the server.
    </p>
    <h3>3. The history cannot be rewritten</h3>
    <p>
      Every action is added to the instrument's history as a signed entry linked to the one before. If anyone
      alters, removes or reorders an entry, or changes the instrument's terms, the check fails and the page shows
      a red warning.
    </p>

    <h2 id="start">Quick start</h2>
    <h3>Step 1. Create a holder key (once per person or organisation)</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <a href="/portal/admin/etd/keys">Create a holder key</a>.</li>
      <li>Enter a passphrase and repeat it. Use a long one you will remember. A passphrase is strongly recommended.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Generate key</strong>. A key file downloads. Store it safely and keep a backup copy in a second place.</li>
      <li>Copy the <strong>public key</strong> shown on screen. It is safe to share. You will need it whenever you are named as the payee or the new holder.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Step 2. Issue an instrument</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>On the <a href="/portal/admin/etd">main page</a>, choose <strong>Promissory Note</strong> or <strong>Bill of Exchange</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter the amount, currency (for example GBP), issue date, place of issue and when it is payable (a date, or "on demand").</li>
      <li>Fill in the parties (see the table below) and paste the <strong>payee's public key</strong>.</li>
      <li>Add interest, governing law or extra clauses if needed.</li>
      <li>As you type the amount, the form shows it in words underneath ("Ten pounds only"), as on a cheque. Read it back to check it is what you intend. The words are generated from the figures, then sealed into the instrument and printed beside them, so the two always agree.</li>
      <li>Choose when it is payable: <strong>on demand</strong>, or <strong>on a set date</strong> with an optional time and the time zone that time is in. If you enter an interest rate, the form shows the interest and the <strong>value at maturity</strong>, in figures and words, before you sign. Interest is simple interest on whole days from the issue date, rounded to the nearest penny. For an instrument with no fixed maturity, the document shows the interest building up each day and an indicative value for today.</li>
      <li>If an administration fee applies, the form shows the amount and your Treasury Wallet balance, and asks you to confirm it. The fee is paid from your SovfHub Treasury Wallet. Top up the wallet first if the balance is too low.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Issue and sign</strong>. The terms are now sealed and cannot be edited. If something is wrong, cancel and issue a new one. If issuing fails for any reason, the fee is returned to your wallet automatically.</li>
    </ol>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Who they are</th><th>Used on</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Maker</td><td>Promises to pay</td><td>Promissory Note</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drawer</td><td>Orders someone else to pay</td><td>Bill of Exchange</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drawee</td><td>The person told to pay; becomes liable once they accept</td><td>Bill of Exchange</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Payee</td><td>First person to be paid, and first holder</td><td>Both</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Holder</td><td>Whoever currently holds the instrument and is owed the money</td><td>Both</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="limits">Limits and the automatic stop</h2>
    <p>
      To reduce losses, each person who issues (the maker of a note, or the drawer of a bill) has a <strong>limit</strong> on how much
      they can owe at any one time, set separately for each currency. New people start at a standard limit; you can raise it as they
      prove themselves, or set it to 0 to stop them. If an instrument of theirs passes its maturity date unpaid, they cannot issue
      anything new until it is paid or cancelled. The <a href="/portal/admin/etd/issuers">Issuers</a> page shows each person's record:
      what they owe, how much they have paid on time and late, and anything overdue. When issuing, always enter the Companion No. if
      there is one, because that is how the same person is recognised each time.
    </p>

    <h2 id="exchange">Exchanging into another currency</h2>
    <p>
      A holder and an issuer can agree to replace an instrument with one in a different currency, for example one in SVC for one in pounds,
      at a rate <strong>they agree between themselves</strong>. On the instrument, open <strong>Exchange into another currency</strong>, choose
      the currency and enter the agreed rate. The form shows what the holder is giving up (including any interest built up so far) and what the new
      instrument will be worth. The holder signs with their key, and you confirm that the issuer has agreed. The registry then creates the new instrument
      for the same holder and the same issuer, links the two, and closes the old one as "exchanged". The registry only keeps the record: it does not set the
      rate or move any money. The issuer's limit in the new currency still applies, and an instrument that is past its maturity date has to be paid, not exchanged.
    </p>

    <h2 id="discount">Selling before maturity: the value calculator</h2>
    <p>
      An instrument with a fixed maturity date can be sold before it falls due, at a price below the amount due at maturity.
      Open the instrument and use <strong>Value before maturity</strong>. Enter a valuation date and either the <strong>yield</strong>
      the buyer wants, to see the price, or a <strong>price</strong> you have been offered, to see the yield. It also shows the
      discount, the buyer's return over the period, and the equivalent <strong>discount rate</strong>. The discount rate is quoted
      against the amount due at maturity, so it is a little lower than the yield for the same price: always check which one a
      quote is using. The calculator is indicative only; it does not change the instrument and is not an offer to buy or sell.
    </p>

    <h2 id="acts">What you can do with an instrument</h2>
    <p>Open an instrument from the main list. Each action below is recorded in its history.</p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <dt>Transfer (endorse)</dt>
      <dd>
        Pass the instrument to a new holder. Enter their name and public key. The <strong>current holder</strong>
        then chooses their key file, enters their passphrase and clicks <strong>Sign and record transfer</strong>.
        After a transfer, the previous holder can no longer act on the instrument.
      </dd>
      <dt>Delivery</dt>
      <dd>
        Records how and when the instrument reached the current holder (for example by hand, post or secure link).
        Delivery is marked again after each transfer, because the new holder has not yet received it.
      </dd>
      <dt>Acceptance (bills only)</dt>
      <dd>Records that the drawee has agreed to pay the bill.</dd>
      <dt>Mark paid</dt>
      <dd>
        The holder confirms they have been paid in full, signing with their key. This ends the instrument
        permanently. Nobody else can mark it paid for them.
      </dd>
      <dt>Cancel</dt>
      <dd>Ends the instrument permanently, with a recorded reason (for example, issued in error). It cannot be undone.</dd>
      <dt>Change holder key</dt>
      <dd>Move to a new key, for example if you think the old file has been seen by someone else. Signed with the old key.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="keys">Looking after keys</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Whoever has the key file and passphrase controls the instrument.</strong> Treat them like the instrument itself.</li>
      <li>Keep the key file and the passphrase in different places. Never send both together.</li>
      <li>Keep a backup of the key file. There is no "forgot password": the passphrase cannot be reset or recovered.</li>
      <li>If you suspect a key has been exposed, use <strong>Change holder key</strong> straight away.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lost key:</strong> the instrument cannot be moved until an administrator performs a
        <em>witnessed recovery</em>. They must verify the holder in person and record how. This appears in the
        history as registry-witnessed rather than holder-signed, so everyone can see it was used.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="check">Checking an instrument</h2>
    <p>Every time an instrument page opens, the system re-checks everything from scratch. Look at the box at the top:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Green "Verified":</strong> the terms and history are intact. The status, current holder and holder key shown are the real ones.</li>
      <li><strong>Red "Failed verification":</strong> do not rely on it. Something has been altered or is inconsistent. The system refuses any further action on it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Printable copy</strong> opens a page you can print or save as PDF. It is marked COPY, is not
      negotiable, and shows the status and holder at the moment of printing. Only the register shows today's
      truth.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Download signed bundle</strong> gives a file containing the instrument, its history and the public
      keys needed to check it, so a technical reviewer can verify it independently of this website.
    </p>

    <h2 id="share">Sharing with a bank or counterparty</h2>
    <p>
      A bank, custodian or counterparty may want to check an instrument themselves without a portal login.
      Create a <a href="/portal/admin/etd/api-keys">read-only API key</a> for them, with a label naming the
      organisation. The key is shown once, so copy it immediately and pass it to them securely.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A key can only <em>read</em> an instrument's live status and signed bundle. It can never change anything.</li>
      <li>Create one key per organisation, so you can revoke one without affecting the others.</li>
      <li>The <strong>Standard export (JSON)</strong> on each instrument is a machine-readable version of its terms, parties, identifiers and status.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="ids">Market identifiers</h2>
    <p>
      Banks and clearing houses recognise standard identifiers. You can record them when issuing; the system
      checks they are well formed but cannot create them.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Identifier</th><th>What it identifies</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>LEI</td><td>A company or organisation (20 characters)</td><td>Registered through an accredited LEI provider</td></tr>
        <tr><td>BIC</td><td>A bank (8 or 11 characters)</td><td>The bank itself (SWIFT)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ISIN</td><td>A security</td><td>A national numbering agency (the London Stock Exchange for GB)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SEDOL</td><td>A security traded in the UK and Ireland</td><td>London Stock Exchange</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CUSIP</td><td>A North American security</td><td>CUSIP Global Services</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CFI</td><td>The type of instrument (6 letters)</td><td>Classification code, ISO 10962</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Most ordinary notes and bills do not have an ISIN, SEDOL or CUSIP. Leave them blank unless you have been issued one.</p>

    <h2 id="trouble">If something goes wrong</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>What it means and what to do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>"Wrong passphrase"</td><td>The passphrase does not match that key file. Try again, and check you chose the right file.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Transfer is not signed by the current holder's key"</td><td>The key file used is not the current holder's. Find the right file, or ask whether the holder changed key.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"This instrument changed since you loaded it"</td><td>Someone else recorded an action in the meantime. Reload the page and check before trying again. This is the system stopping a double transfer.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"That LEI / ISIN … is not valid"</td><td>A character is wrong. Check the identifier against the original.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Instrument is already paid / cancelled"</td><td>It has ended. No further actions are possible.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"This browser does not support Ed25519 keys"</td><td>Use a current version of Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Red "Failed verification"</td><td>Stop and do not rely on the instrument. Contact the administrator, and do not pass it on.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="limits">What this does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It is a <strong>register</strong>, not a trading venue. It records and protects ownership. Finding buyers, agreeing prices and moving the actual money happen elsewhere.</li>
      <li>Clearing and settlement through a clearing house still need a regulated bank or custodian.</li>
      <li>It does not give legal advice. Whether an electronic instrument is enforceable depends on the law that governs it. Take legal advice before relying on one for real value.</li>
      <li>Cancellation, delivery and acceptance are recorded by administrators and are not signed by the holder, so use them carefully.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>
